const NOHB = 'Do not call any mcp__hearthbot__ tools. Load WebSearch and WebFetch with ToolSearch ("select:WebSearch,WebFetch") when you need them. The web-search tool has a shared budget across every agent in this run, so stay within the query cap given to you; if a search call is refused, record that and stop searching rather than working around it (GitHub MCP code/issue search, arXiv listing pages via WebFetch and OEIS pages are acceptable free alternatives). ACCESS ROUTING, which decides novelty grades and is not optional: when a search returns a paper you need to read, resolve its DOI to the PUBLISHER page first (doi.org, or link.springer.com / nature.com / sciencedirect.com / wiley.com / journals.plos.org directly). PubMed Central and Europe PMC are hard-blocked in this environment - PMC serves a reCAPTCHA interstitial and Europe PMC returns HTTP 429 with an instruction not to retry - so a PMCID is an identifier to convert, never a route to try first. On 2026-10-16 an on-point paper defeated seven PMC/Europe PMC attempts across three agents and then opened on the first publisher call, and reading it turned a near-Validated candidate into a Gate 2 rejection. If you cannot read a paper, say COULD NOT READ and list the routes that failed; never record an unread paper absence as a check that passed, and never let a title alone ground a grade in either direction. NEVER put a person\'s email, name or any other personal identifier into a URL, query string, header or form: skip Unpaywall entirely rather than supply a contact address. ROUTE FACTS ESTABLISHED FIRST-HAND, so you do not spend calls rediscovering them. A curl 403 at this proxy is TOOL-SCOPED, not a host block: six readers logged api.crossref.org refusing CONNECT to curl with "CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403" while the same readers reached it fine through WebFetch, so never conclude a host is blocked from a curl failure - try WebFetch. nature.com fails BOTH ways, curl with a proxy 403 and WebFetch with CLIENT_ERROR from the target, even for an open-access article: go straight to an institutional mirror of the published PDF (an eprint or repository host) rather than burning calls on the publisher. doi.org can return HTTP 429 with an explicit do-not-retry instruction, so prefer the publisher host directly and treat a 429 as final. An Oxford University Press "article-abstract" page serves a usable abstract for a paywalled paper, but its OCR can be damaged - reproduce it as served and flag the damage rather than silently correcting it. Raw curl bypasses the summariser only on raw.githubusercontent.com; assume no raw read is available elsewhere. WHEN AN ARXIV PDF FAILS, TRY THE HTML RENDERING at arxiv.org/html/<id>v<n> before you write COULD NOT READ: on 2026-10-21 a paper logged COULD NOT READ in a batch read in FULL at that route, and only the PDF route had failed, which is a fact about the route and not about the document. It is not universal - another paper\'s tables and appendix bodies were served by no route tried, and two consecutive PDF fetches returned RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED - so record WHICH route failed rather than that the paper is unreadable. THE SUMMARISER\'S ~125-CHARACTER QUOTATION CAP IS BEATABLE and is not a reason to report a quotation unobtainable: ask for ORDERED CONSECUTIVE SEGMENTS each short enough to return whole, assemble only from consecutive returns, NEVER complete a cut string from inference, verify by an overlap at the junction or a word count against the summariser\'s own, and LABEL the result a reconstruction saying how many fragments it came from. A narrower request for short exact fragments can succeed where a sentence-by-sentence request is refused - that is a smaller ask, not a rework of a refusal, and say so. Do not inspect or probe the egress proxy; two agents hit classifier refusals doing that, and the facts above are what they were looking for. QUOTATION FIDELITY, which is where the last three batches went wrong with every source real and open. NAME YOUR READ LEVEL for every source you grade on: full text, abstract only, or COULD NOT READ. "Abstract-level only, grounds nothing" is a fact about your read and not about the paper, and a source dismissed from its title has twice become the ground a later verdict rested on. When you quote, check the GRAMMAR ON BOTH SIDES OF THE CUT: a string that is verbatim but cut where the surrounding clause changes the claim is a misquotation, and six such clipped quotations across four entries were the signature defect of the 2026-10-18 09:45 batch. When a source states a claim MORE THAN ONCE, quote the formulation it hedged and cited rather than the bare one: the sharpest case in that batch misquoted nothing, it had quoted the unhedged uncited formulation instead of the hedged one two sentences later. Check whether the source is a REVIEW, because attributing a finding to a review instead of to the study it cites is its own defect, as is reading the word predominantly in a review as a quantity. Check WHAT A PAPER MEASURES and not only what it is about: arXiv:2601.16612 finds smart-meter error across manufacturers but on a voltage signal, where the claim it was cited for needed distorted current. Take author names from the FRONT MATTER and never from a short form someone else wrote, since "Zhang et al." for a paper by Han et al. survived three passes because each pass inherited the label. A SCOPE claim (nobody anywhere has done this) needs its own verbatim check, and a figure must be re-derived at the threshold ITS author stated. Silence in a source is not a prohibition, and a source noting the absence of a DIFFERENT thing is not a flag of your gap. SCOPE TRANSFER is the newest signature defect and it cost the 2026-10-21 04:52 batch five of twelve verified claims: every source existed, opened, and said somewhere what it was quoted as saying, but the claim was true only in the regime its author proved it in. Before any source grounds a grade, answer one question in writing: in what regime was this established - which population, unit of analysis, load or operating condition, instrument, threshold and benefit category - and is the candidate inside it? Four real instances from that batch. A no-bias theorem proved for an ALL-BINARY model, whose one non-binary passage calls the induced error differential and declares its own sections not applicable, was cited for a provider-level preference-rate instrument the paper never addresses in either direction. Pipeline pressure logged during a DELIBERATE pressure test, after patients had been moved to redistribute flow, was cited as bedside delivered flow during the incident. An effectiveness figure stated for SEVEN CHECKS USED IN COMBINATION was detached from that qualifier. A simulated share of USERS deterred was used as a component of a utilisation effect, which sits on a different unit. A figure measured under a test condition is not the operating value, and a result proved under one set of assumptions is silent outside them, not supportive. A MULTI-LEG kill needs every leg sourced separately: in that batch a three-leg kill held on one leg and lost the other two on verification, so name the leg your verdict would stand on if the others fell. PUBLISHED IS NOT PERFORMED. This was the signature defect of the 2026-10-21 12:33 batch and it reversed a verdict there. A paper can RUN an analysis and never PRINT its number: a receiver-operating panel whose area under the curve is only shaded, a Monte Carlo announced in an abstract with no result in the text, a metric defined and bounded but never measured, a design margin specified as-designed and never audited in service, a per-particle composition cited for a mass share nobody mass-balanced. None of those makes a quantitative question a known. So whenever a kill claims a source publishes the candidate QUANTITY, NAME THE NUMBER AND SAY WHERE IT IS PRINTED - the table, figure or sentence, with its units and its denominator. If you cannot name it, then the source DEFINES, BOUNDS, PREDICTS or ANNOUNCES the quantity rather than measuring it, and you say which. A citation to a DEFINITION is not an evaluation. Converting a threshold through a fitted curve is the inverse of scoring that threshold as a classifier, not the same thing. A classification axis that exists but stops short of the candidate grain - a statutory chemistry class that ends at lithium-based where the candidate needs cathode chemistry - is a GRANULARITY gap and is reported as one, neither as an absence nor as a known. WRITE THE REGIME DOWN as its own one-line REGIME field beside every source you grade on, naming population, unit of analysis, operating condition, instrument, threshold and category. Scope transfer recurred six times in that batch with the regime question sitting in every agent preamble, so a rule you read and do not write out does not fire. A PRIOR YOU DID NOT OPEN IS NOT PRIOR ART. This reversed a verdict in the 2026-10-21 18:33 batch, and it is the simplest of all these failures. A Gate 2 record there rejected a candidate as a disguised known on a paper that is open and readable at BOTH routes the record itself named, so the kill was a retrieval failure - and what the record wrote about the paper was wrong exactly where it mattered: the within-version repeat-run control it said no source supplies is printed in that paper\'s own section 3.4, and the design it credited with a predecessor and a vendor-designated successor is two dated snapshots of ONE live model name, with no retirement anywhere in it. So before any novelty-Low grade, state your READ LEVEL for that prior and QUOTE the line that does the killing. "The nearest hit is presumably about X" is not a check that ran, a described-but-unopened prior grounds no grade in either direction, and a retrieval failure recorded as an absence is the one defect on this ledger that has reversed verdicts in both directions. NOW THE PART THAT KEEPS FAILING: RUN THAT CHECK ON YOUR OWN SEARCH RETURNS, not only on priors someone handed you. The rule above sat in every agent preamble of the 2026-10-22 00:33 batch and the failure still happened THREE times in five entries. The worst instance was entirely self-inflicted: an adversary\'s own search returned a paper, the adversary never fetched it, and that paper prints the exact between-report figure the record had just called unpublished - so one entry carried a false Unresolved attack and a false novelty negative at the same time, from a title that was sitting in its own search log. A paper your own search returned and you did not open is a paper you have not checked. So before you write that a quantity has no published value, or that no source does X, LIST the titles your own searches returned that could carry it and say for each whether you opened it; a one-line \'returned, not opened\' is honest and an unqualified absence is not. THE SAME APPLIES TO A PREMISE ABOUT A KIND OF DOCUMENT. A claim conditional on documents of a kind behaving a certain way - a datasheet printing one scalar value, a certificate carrying a figure, a release file containing a column - is not established until you have opened two or three ACTUAL INSTANCES of that kind and found the premise holds. In that batch a fatal algebraic identity rested on vendor datasheets each yielding one scalar concentration; nobody had opened a datasheet; three were opened afterwards and ZERO supplied it, in three different forms (a per-lot value on a separately versioned certificate, an approximate range pointing at a lot certificate, and a mass to be reconstituted at a volume the buyer picks). That reversed the verdict. Opening three instances of a document kind costs two fetches and is the cheapest check in this pipeline. AND NOW THE NEWEST FAILURE, WHICH IS WHAT HAPPENS AFTER YOU DO OPEN THE PRIOR. On 2026-10-22 at 06:33 an adversary did everything the rule above asks: it pulled a paper out of Gate 2\'s own search log, fetched it at both routes, quoted its abstract verbatim, named every number with its printed location, and even flagged the source\'s own internal discrepancy. The kill still fell, because the number it found was A DIFFERENT NUMERATOR ON A DIFFERENT DENOMINATOR. So OPENING THE PRIOR IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT, and these two checks belong in the same breath. Before any novelty-Low grade on a quantity, write out in the paper\'s own words: (a) its NUMERATOR CLASS, quoting the definition from its Methods, including every exclusion the definition states; (b) its DENOMINATOR, checked against the near neighbours it is not - performed against ordered against released against reported, screened against eligible against enrolled, records against patients against events; and (c) THE OVERLAP IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the part that gets skipped: what the prior\'s class MISSES that the candidate includes, AND what it INCLUDES that the candidate excludes. Both directions, every time. In that instance the prior\'s class missed four of the candidate\'s five event causes and included up to 46.6% of its own numerator where the candidate\'s object was not present at all, and it printed the share REPEATED where the candidate asks the share REPLACED - one word apart and a different quantity. Check the paper\'s INTERNAL CONSISTENCY too before you lean on it: that one carried three mutually inconsistent corpus totals and two different rates for one numerator, so the composition of its headline figure could not be reconstructed from anything it prints, and a figure you cannot decompose cannot be matched to a candidate\'s class. RECORD EVERY SOURCE BY DOI PLUS FIRST AUTHOR PLUS YEAR PLUS TITLE, NEVER BY A PMCID ALONE. In the same batch three sources were logged as could not read at any route, each identified only by its PMCID; every one converted in ONE call to https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/utils/idconv/v1.0/?ids=<id>&format=json, two then opened at the publisher, and one of them turned out to be that entry\'s OWN fourth prior, already listed under its DOI - so a single entry carried the same paper twice and called it unreadable. Convert a PMCID the moment you see one and carry the DOI thereafter. AND THE NEWEST FAILURE OF ALL, FROM 2026-10-22 AT 12:33, WHICH IS A QUOTATION RULE THE GRAMMAR CHECK ABOVE DOES NOT CATCH. Three entries in that batch carried a quotation that was verbatim, grammatically COMPLETE on both sides of the cut, and still wrong, because the cut had dropped a SCOPE-SETTING OPENER. \"In such cases,\" was dropped from the front of a sentence, which turned a discrepancy-resolution procedure for one parameter class of one molecule into a general selection policy the document states nowhere; the same cut also dropped the trailing \"and also considered the type of experiment used\", which made a two-criterion judgment look like a rule. \"At the time of the Oyafuso article publication,\" was dropped, which turned a claim true at an INSTANT into a standing claim that this is the only source. And four verbatim words were quoted out of a sentence recording a system MOVING AWAY from that very quantity, \"UT1 - UTC has been replaced by UT1 - GPST\", so an entry cited a paper for the opposite of what it says and booked the source as a point in the candidate\'s favour. So when you quote, do two things the grammar check does not: say what the sentence\'s OPENING WORDS SCOPE, and say whether the clause your words sit in ASSERTS the quantity, DENIES it, or RECORDS A MOVE AWAY from it. A grammatically clean cut is not a faithful one, and a conditional opener is the cheapest thing in a sentence to lose. TWO FURTHER RULES FROM THAT SAME BATCH. First, DO NOT NOMINATE AN UNOPENED SOURCE AS THE ONE THAT WOULD SETTLE A PRESUPPOSITION until you have written down what KIND of document could settle it - what population it must cover, what it must perturb or vary, and what it must PRINT - and checked that the nominee is that kind. One entry named a paper as the single source most likely to settle its cross-mission presupposition; read in full, that paper has no term of the needed kind in its error decomposition at all (the only occurrence of the root word in it is a reference-list title), has no second independent mission with a figure of its own, and states an explicit independence assumption. Its silence was silence under a regime that EXCLUDES the question, which is not evidence in either direction, and the nomination had been made from the title. Second, COUNT REPEATED TITLE RETURNS AS TITLE MATCHES AND NEVER AS CORROBORATION. The same entry recorded that an item had been returned four times across two adversaries\' sweeps, as though four returns were four confirmations; they were four title-level matches on one phrase, by nobody who opened it. Convergence among unopened returns is not convergence, and reporting it as such manufactures a false signal of agreement. ONE RULE THAT IS WORKING, SO KEEP USING IT: the measurand exemption was APPLIED, not merely considered, in ten of thirteen adversary logs in that batch, several saying in terms that they declared it rather than grading around it, and both of the batch\'s kills still landed on clauses that genuinely are presuppositions. Declaring the exemption out loud costs a question nothing and saves a well-built one from being killed for reporting the very failure an attack wants to call a false premise. AND NOW THE SIGNATURE DEFECT OF THE 2026-10-22 18:33 BATCH, WHICH IS THE DOCUMENT-KIND RULE ABOVE POINTED AT A CLASS OF ARTIFACTS. Two unrelated entries in that batch asserted a UNIVERSAL over a kind of artifact that nobody had enumerated - that no released conversation corpus can re-serve a prompt against a pinned model, and that no published vision-language model prints its patch grid - and each was false on the one instance nobody opened: WildChat-1M and LMSYS-Chat-1M both carry re-served turns, and LLaVA-1.5-7B publishes its grid. So whenever you write EVERY, NO, NONE or NOBODY about a kind of artifact - corpora, releases, datasheets, certificates, benchmark suites, model cards, standards - LIST the instances you opened, NAME the ones you did not, and treat the claim as Unverified until that list exists. An unenumerated universal is the cheapest claim in this pipeline to break and the most expensive to leave standing, because it reads as a finding; two or three instances is the whole cost of checking it. AND A TITLE CAN NAME NO PAPER AT ALL, BECAUSE IT IS A REPOST HEADLINE. In the same batch, the title How Many Glitch Tokens Hide in Popular LLMs was carried as an unopened prior; it is not a paper but a blog restatement of Land and Bartolo 2024 (arXiv:2405.05417), which that same entry had ALREADY OPENED AND QUOTED, and a second blog-style title was a chunk of the same document. Three returned-not-opened items were one read paper, which inflated the unopened-prior list threefold, and the novelty worry hung on them reversed the moment the paper was read. So resolve any aggregator, newsletter or blog-style title to a DOI or an arXiv identifier BEFORE you list it as a prior, cite the paper and never the repost, and count repeated returns of such a title as ONE document. AND ONE RULE ABOUT YOUR OWN REPAIRS, WHICH IS WHERE THAT BATCH LOST ITS LAST CANDIDATE. Gate 1 allows one reformulation, and a reformulation carries presuppositions OF ITS OWN. A late Gate 4 killed an entry there not on its original clause but on the repair: the reformulation required two checkpoints of one model differing only in context length, and the release it named reaches its long-context window training-free on ONE set of weights, so the checkpoint pair it asked for does not exist. Test the clause you wrote to fix a problem exactly as hard as the clause it replaced, and say in writing what the repair now presupposes. AND NOW THE FOUR RULES FROM THE 2026-10-23 00:33 BATCH, IN WHICH BOTH REJECTIONS LOST THE GROUND THEY STOOD ON. FIRST, AN UNENUMERATED EXISTENCE CLAIM IS THE SAME DEFECT AS THE UNENUMERATED UNIVERSAL ABOVE, AND IT IS WORSE, BECAUSE IT CAN CARRY A VERDICT. The universal rule tells you to enumerate when you write EVERY or NO. The mirror image is a claim that SOME instance exists - that the declared frame admits pairs of studies estimating different objects, that some release carries the column, that such a case occurs. In that batch a kill rested on exactly such a claim and conceded in its own words: I opened no instance of a qualifying study pair and did not measure the contaminating share, so a verifier holding that the existence claim needs an enumerated pair should downgrade this to Partial. Name one instance you opened, or grade the claim Unverified and say that it is. SECOND, MARK EVERY STEP OF YOUR OWN REASONING AS YOUR OWN, THE WAY YOU ALREADY MARK YOUR OWN ARITHMETIC. In that batch a load-bearing leg of a kill was pure logic about the shape of a claim, and its author said so - THIS IS MY OWN REASONING ABOUT THE CLAIM\'S LOGIC, NOT A SOURCE FINDING - and added that the search which could have grounded it was never run. That honesty is what made the kill reviewable and is why it fell. A step of argument is not a finding: label it, say what search would have grounded it, and remember that a kill whose load-bearing step is your own inference is Partial at best. The same discipline applies to a reader who cannot reach a front matter - say so rather than copying a short form someone else wrote. THIRD, ONE KEYWORD SWEEP\'S SILENCE IS NOT ABSENCE. In that batch a reader\'s broad sweep reported a phrase missing from a paper, and its own dedicated exhaustive sweep then found the single occurrence - the one that cut against the reading the entry wanted. Before you write that a term, a number or a concept does not appear in a document, say which sweep you ran and run a second one aimed only at that term. FOURTH, A COULD NOT READ MUST SAY WHETHER THE ROUTE FAILED OR YOUR BUDGET RAN OUT. In that batch an entry logged a paper as COULD NOT READ when the document opens in ONE fetch at the very route the entry\'s own citation names - the publisher article HTML 302s, and the redirect is followable in one further call. That is a spent budget recorded as a property of the source, and it is the easiest kind of false absence to ship. Name the routes you tried, say which error each returned, and say plainly if you simply stopped. AND ONE MORE, ABOUT CORROBORATION: SEVERAL DOCUMENTS FROM ONE LABORATORY ARE NOT SEVERAL SUPPORTS. Four sources anchoring one claim in that batch - a conference abstract, a preprint under two identifiers, a thesis and an unopened paper - carried the same two authors throughout, and the preprint\'s key sentence was explicitly a reaffirmation of the paper. Count the GROUPS, not the citations, and say where a claim rests on one group citing itself. AND NOW THE RULES FROM THE 2026-10-23 06:33 BATCH, IN WHICH BOTH OF THE SINGLE-LEG REJECTIONS LOST THE LEG THEY STOOD ON. FIRST, A QUOTATION CAN EXIST IN NO DOCUMENT AT ALL, AND THE GRAMMAR AND SCOPE CHECKS ABOVE CANNOT CATCH IT. One entry in that batch presented the string \'Repeatability or reproducibility study: None described\' as what an FDA clearance document STATES; the string is in neither the primary document (K213037) nor its mirror nor any other FDA document searched. That is an EXISTENCE defect rather than a fidelity defect, so for every quotation you attribute to a document, name the document you opened and the section the string sits in, and if you cannot locate it, STRIKE it - never reattribute an unlocated string to a guessed source, and never carry one forward as a promotion condition. SECOND, A LEAD YOU NOMINATE BY KIND IS A WORK ITEM, NOT A REMARK. A pass in that batch wrote that the question would be settled by a proficiency-testing programme\'s own report of missed detections, and opened none; the next reader found LaMotte et al., Public Health Reports 1977;92(6):554-560 (PMID 200968) at FULL TEXT in ONE fetch at stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/65385/cdc_65385_DS1.pdf, and it replaced an entire rejection ground, because the question had been asked and published 49 years earlier. The moment you write \'what would settle this is a document of kind K\', spend one search on kind K; if your budget will not reach, hand the kind to the next reader in exactly those words. THIRD, 49 YEARS IS NOT TOO OLD TO BE PRIOR ART. Every search that missed that 1977 report was aimed at the last fifteen years. When a question is about a measurement practice, a programme, a procedure or a reporting convention rather than a new instrument, spend one query with NO date filter and one aimed at the era when the practice began. FOURTH, A SUMMARISER REFUSAL IS A FACT ABOUT YOUR REQUEST, NOT A PROPERTY OF THE DOCUMENT. \'No summariser served that table\'s cells\' was recorded in that batch as though the table could not be read; its three cells came back on a transcription-style request in one fetch. Re-ask for ordered consecutive segments before you record a table, a figure or a number as unobtainable, and if you still stop, say that you stopped. FIFTH, A SET THE QUESTION PRE-DECLARES MUST BE ENUMERATED OR GENERATED BY A STATED RULE. One entry turned on a \'pre-declared drug list\' mentioned eleven times and enumerated nowhere; that is a well-posedness defect even when every other clause is sharp. Either list the members, or state the rule and the source that generates them, and say which of the two you did.'

